      "id": 1,
      "text": "First Mesa was founded by Mormons on February 14, 1878, marking the start of the town’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mesaaz.gov/Government/About-Mesa/Mesa-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers tapped an abandoned Hohokam canal, the Mesa Canal, to irrigate the first farms in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mesaaz.gov/Government/About-Mesa/Mesa-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rail service arrived in December 1895 when the Maricopa & Phoenix & Salt River Valley Railroad linked Mesa to Tempe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mesaaz.gov/Government/About-Mesa/Mesa-History",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Sacred Heart mission, founded by Catholics in 1908, later became the Mezona church, a blend of Mesa and Arizona.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mesaaz.gov/Government/About-Mesa/Mesa-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1914, the University of Arizona established a research station on Main Street that tested new crops for local farmers until 1983.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
